/* CyberShield Academy: a plain-language path from first principles to defensive practice. */
(function() {
    function lesson(title, level, read, example, practice, question, answer) {
        return {
            title: title,
            level: level,
            minutes: level === "Advanced" ? 8 : level === "Intermediate" ? 6 : 4,
            read: read.split("|"),
            example: example,
            practice: practice,
            question: question,
            answer: answer
        };
    }

    window.cyberShieldCurriculum = [
        {
            id: "networking",
            title: "Networking Essentials",
            category: "FOUNDATION",
            description: "Start with how devices communicate, then build toward routing, DNS, segmentation, and defensive traffic analysis.",
            diagram: { kind: "flow", accent: "#55c4ff", caption: "A request travels through several trusted steps.", nodes: ["YOUR DEVICE", "WI-FI / ROUTER", "DNS LOOKUP", "WEB SERVER"] },
            references: [],
            lessons: [
                lesson("What is a network?", "Beginner", "A network is a group of devices that exchange information using agreed rules. Your phone, laptop, and router form a small local network; the internet is many connected networks.|Information is split into manageable pieces called packets. Each packet carries addressing information so network equipment can move it toward the right destination. A network is not just cables: it includes devices, software, rules, and people who manage access.", "When you open a learning video, your phone sends a request through Wi-Fi and receives many small packets that the browser assembles into a picture and sound.", "Draw your own home network using only devices you own. Mark which device connects you to the internet; do not inspect anyone else's traffic.", "Why are packets useful?", "They can be routed and retransmitted in manageable pieces, instead of treating all communication as one enormous block."),
                lesson("Devices and connections", "Beginner", "A switch connects devices inside a local network. A router moves traffic between networks. A wireless access point provides Wi-Fi; many home routers combine all three jobs.|A modem or fiber terminal links your home equipment to an internet provider. These devices have different roles even when they share one plastic box. Knowing the role helps you find where a connectivity problem might be.", "If a laptop can reach a printer on Wi-Fi but not a public website, the local connection may work while the router or provider connection has a problem.", "Label the router, access point, and internet connection in a diagram. Use the device labels or manual rather than changing settings.", "Which device commonly moves traffic from your home network to another network?", "A router. A switch mainly connects devices within one local network."),
                lesson("IP addresses in everyday terms", "Beginner", "An IP address lets network traffic identify a source and destination. IPv4 addresses look like four numbers; IPv6 uses longer hexadecimal groups and provides a much larger address space.|Private addresses are used inside local networks. A router may translate many private devices to one public address. DHCP can assign local addresses automatically, while DNS helps people use names instead of memorizing addresses.", "Your laptop can have a private address such as 192.168.1.25 while your home router uses a public address to communicate with the provider.", "On a device you own, view its network settings and identify whether the address is private. Do not post your public address or network details online.", "Does an IP address prove who a person is?", "No. It identifies a network endpoint at a moment in time; shared networks, proxies, and reassignment make identity conclusions unreliable."),
                lesson("Ports and protocols", "Beginner", "A protocol is a shared set of communication rules. TCP provides ordered delivery and retries; UDP sends datagrams with less delivery coordination. Apps choose based on what they need.|A port is a numbered endpoint used by software on a device. A port number alone does not tell you whether a service is safe. A firewall can allow or deny traffic based on rules, and exposed services should be intentional and maintained.", "A browser commonly uses HTTPS over TCP port 443, while real-time voice systems may use UDP. These are common patterns, not a guarantee about every service.", "Read a service's official documentation before changing a firewall. In a diagram, show how an allowed rule differs from a blocked rule.", "What is the difference between a protocol and a port?", "A protocol defines communication rules; a port helps the operating system deliver traffic to the right software endpoint."),
                lesson("How the web connects", "Beginner", "When you enter a domain, the browser first needs to find an address using DNS. It then establishes a connection to the server and, for HTTPS, negotiates encryption and checks the server certificate.|The browser sends an HTTP request and receives a response containing a page or an error. The page may request images, scripts, and styles as separate resources. HTTPS protects information in transit, but it does not prove that a site is honest.", "A padlock on a bank's real domain means the connection is encrypted to that domain. A look-alike domain can also use HTTPS, so read the domain carefully.", "Trace the steps for a site you trust: domain name, DNS, HTTPS connection, request, response. Avoid entering account details on an unfamiliar site.", "What does HTTPS help protect?", "It encrypts data in transit and helps authenticate the server endpoint; it does not judge the site's intentions."),
                lesson("Networking recap", "Intermediate", "A message may cross your device, access point, router, provider networks, and a server. Addressing and routing help move it; protocols define how endpoints exchange data.|Troubleshooting works best when you change one variable at a time. Ask whether the device is connected, the name resolves, the route exists, and the service responds. Record observations before changing a setting.", "If a website name fails but a known IP address works, name resolution may be involved. This is a clue to investigate, not proof of the cause.", "Write a five-step checklist for diagnosing your own internet connection. Include a stop point before resetting or factory-restoring equipment.", "Why should troubleshooting change one thing at a time?", "It preserves clues and makes it easier to identify which change affected the result."),
                lesson("Subnets and CIDR", "Intermediate", "A subnet is a smaller logical range inside an IP network. CIDR notation such as /24 says how many leading bits identify the network; the remaining bits identify addresses within that range.|For IPv4, a /24 contains 256 total address values, though the usable host count depends on the subnet type and reserved addresses. IPv6 uses different conventions. Subnets support organization and policy boundaries; the math should be checked with a trusted calculator or course lab.", "A small office might separate guest Wi-Fi from staff devices so visitor traffic cannot casually reach internal printers or workstations.", "Use an offline subnet calculator with a made-up private address, then explain what the prefix controls. Do not scan or probe a network.", "What does the /24 in CIDR describe?", "The network prefix length in bits; it is not a count of 24 devices."),
                lesson("DNS records and resolution", "Advanced", "DNS is a distributed naming system. A resolver follows records to find information such as an A or AAAA address, a CNAME alias, MX mail servers, or TXT policy data.|Caching improves speed but means an update may take time to appear everywhere. DNSSEC can help validate that DNS data was not altered in transit, when the domain and resolver support it. DNS is separate from HTTPS: one finds a destination; the other protects a web connection.", "A company may use an A record for its website, MX records for mail, and TXT records to publish email anti-spoofing policies.", "Sketch a DNS lookup for a domain you own or a fictional domain. Label the resolver and the record type without changing real DNS settings.", "Does a correct DNS answer mean the website itself is trustworthy?", "No. It only helps locate a destination. You still need to verify the domain, purpose, and connection."),
                lesson("Network segmentation and firewall thinking", "Advanced", "Segmentation divides a network into zones with different trust levels. A firewall enforces allowed communication between them. The safest default is to permit only required flows and review rules when systems change.|Good rules identify source, destination, service, owner, reason, and expiry or review date. Broad rules create hidden pathways. Monitoring helps detect unexpected communication, but alerting should be paired with a response plan.", "A guest Wi-Fi zone usually needs internet access but not access to staff laptops or internal administration systems.", "On paper, list three zones for a small library and one necessary connection between each pair. This is a design exercise, not a network change.", "Why is a narrowly scoped rule easier to defend?", "It exposes fewer systems, has a clear purpose, and is easier to review when that purpose ends."),
                lesson("Reading traffic safely", "Advanced", "A packet capture can reveal timestamps, addresses, protocol metadata, and sometimes unencrypted content. Captures may contain personal data, so collect only with permission and protect or delete them according to the lab rules.|Defenders use captures to answer a specific question, such as whether a device repeatedly retries a connection. Start with a written question, a short time window, and a lab you control. Do not intercept other people's Wi-Fi or decrypt traffic without explicit authorization.", "In your own isolated lab, compare a normal DNS lookup with a failed lookup and note only the timestamps and response type.", "Use a provided sample capture or a local training lab. Before capturing anything, write down the scope, purpose, data handling plan, and stop time.", "What should you decide before starting a capture?", "The authorized scope, question, data to collect, storage and deletion plan, and when to stop.")
            ]
        },
        {
            id: "linux",
            title: "Linux Fundamentals",
            category: "FOUNDATION",
            description: "Learn the shell, files, permissions, processes, logs, and safe system administration habits.",
            diagram: { kind: "stack", accent: "#65dfb5", caption: "Commands pass through layers to reach files and devices.", nodes: ["YOU", "SHELL", "KERNEL", "FILES + DEVICES"] },
            references: [{ label: "Linux manual pages", url: "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/" }],
            lessons: [
                lesson("Meet the command line", "Beginner", "A terminal gives you a text-based way to ask the operating system to perform tasks. The shell reads a command, finds a program, supplies its arguments, and shows the result.|Begin with observation commands and read the manual before trying unfamiliar options. A prompt is not a magic box: commands can change files, permissions, and services, so understand what a command will do first.", "In a practice folder, pwd shows the current location and ls lists nearby names. These commands observe; they do not modify files.", "Open a terminal and run pwd, then ls in a folder you own. If a command is unfamiliar, consult its manual or built-in help.", "What does the shell do?", "It interprets your command line and starts the requested program with its arguments."),
                lesson("Navigate files and folders", "Beginner", "Linux organizes files in a tree that begins at /. A path beginning with / is absolute; a path without it is interpreted from your current folder.|Use cd to move and mkdir to create a folder you own. Names with spaces need quoting. Before removing or moving files, print the path and make sure it is the item you intended.", "A safe practice path is a new folder inside your home directory, not a system directory.", "Create a temporary practice folder, move into it, and verify the path with pwd. Do not use recursive delete commands while learning.", "What does an absolute path begin with on Linux?", "A forward slash, representing the root of the file system."),
                lesson("Read and organize files", "Beginner", "Small text files can be displayed with cat or inspected a page at a time with less. head and tail show the beginning or end. cp copies, while mv renames or moves.|Redirection sends output to a file and pipes pass output between programs. These are powerful building blocks; inspect both the input and destination before redirecting or overwriting data.", "A command that reads a sample file and pipes it to a text search tool can help you find one line without changing the original.", "Create a sample note in your practice folder, copy it, and compare both files. Use harmless sample text, not personal logs.", "What does a pipe do?", "It sends one program's output to another program as input."),
                lesson("Users and permissions", "Beginner", "Linux separates user identities and file permissions. Read, write, and execute rights can be set for an owner, a group, and others. The least privilege principle means giving only the rights required for a task.|A permission that is too broad can expose or alter files. sudo runs an approved command with elevated rights, so treat it as a safety boundary, not a shortcut. Never change ownership or permissions on system files just to make an error disappear.", "A private journal should not be readable by every account on a shared computer.", "Use ls -l to inspect a sample file in your own folder. Explain who can read it before changing anything.", "Why is using sudo for every command risky?", "It gives routine commands more authority than necessary, so a mistake can affect the whole system."),
                lesson("Linux recap", "Intermediate", "A safe workflow is: confirm your location, inspect the target, understand the command, make one limited change, and verify the result. Small reversible exercises build reliable habits.|The same principle applies to servers: work under an approved account, keep a change record, and ask before touching shared systems. Command-line skill includes knowing when not to run something.", "Create and read a sample file in your own folder, then remove only that named sample after checking the path.", "Write down what each command in your practice sequence does before pressing Enter.", "What is the most important check before a destructive command?", "Confirm the exact target and scope, and make sure you have a safe recovery path."),
                lesson("Processes and services", "Intermediate", "A process is a running program. A service is usually a long-running process managed by the operating system. Process tools show what is running; service managers show whether an approved service is active.|Do not stop an unfamiliar service on a shared or production system. First learn its owner, purpose, dependencies, and change procedure. In a lab, observation is safer than experimenting on a machine that supports other people.", "A web server may run as a service and write status messages to a system log.", "On your own Linux lab, list processes and inspect the status of a known service. Do not stop or disable it.", "What extra role does a service manager provide?", "It supervises services, starts or stops them under policy, and reports their status."),
                lesson("Logs and troubleshooting", "Advanced", "Logs are time-ordered records that help explain system behavior. A useful investigation correlates timestamps, service names, and related events rather than treating one line as a complete explanation.|Logs can contain usernames, addresses, or tokens. Limit access, redact sensitive values before sharing, and use the smallest time window that answers the question. Preserve original evidence when following an incident process.", "If a service fails at 10:15, compare its log around that time with a recent approved configuration change.", "Read a sample log from a training environment and write a timeline. Replace personal details with placeholders before sharing it.", "Why are timestamps and context important in logs?", "They help connect related events and distinguish cause from coincidence."),
                lesson("Shell pipelines without surprises", "Advanced", "Pipelines combine focused tools, but an incorrect assumption can hide errors or produce misleading results. Read each stage from left to right: where data comes from, what is filtered, and where the result goes.|Before automating a pipeline, test it on sample data, quote paths, and handle failures explicitly. Avoid copying commands from strangers without understanding them; a pasted one-liner may delete, upload, or execute content.", "A safe sample pipeline can filter a local text file for the word ERROR and count matching lines.", "Write a pipeline using a tiny file you created yourself. Test without redirection first, then inspect the output.", "Why test a pipeline on sample data first?", "It reveals incorrect filters or side effects before the command touches important information."),
                lesson("Updates and least privilege", "Advanced", "System updates include bug fixes and security fixes. Use the operating system's official package manager and trusted repositories. Check release notes and maintenance windows on shared systems before changing installed software.|Run accounts, services, and files with only the permissions they need. Remove unused software and accounts through an approved process. On managed devices, follow the administrator's update policy instead of bypassing it.", "A personal lab can be updated from its normal distribution repository, then restarted when you can safely test that it still works.", "On your own machine, identify the official update tool and find its help page. Do not install packages from random download links.", "What is the security benefit of least privilege?", "A compromised process or mistaken command has fewer permissions and a smaller possible impact.")
            ]
        },
        {
            id: "security",
            title: "Security Foundations",
            category: "SECURITY",
            description: "Build a defender's mental model: assets, risk, identity, resilience, cryptography, and response.",
            diagram: { kind: "triangle", accent: "#b49aff", caption: "Security decisions balance confidentiality, integrity, and availability.", nodes: ["CONFIDENTIALITY", "INTEGRITY", "AVAILABILITY"] },
            references: [{ label: "NIST Cybersecurity Framework", url: "https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework" }],
            lessons: [
                lesson("The security mindset", "Beginner", "Security reduces risk; it cannot promise that risk is zero. Start by identifying an asset, the people or services that depend on it, what could go wrong, and how severe the outcome would be.|Choose safeguards that fit the risk and the people using the system. A complicated control that everyone bypasses may work worse than a simpler control used consistently. Review assumptions when the system or threat changes.", "For a school account, the asset is access to learning records; a lost password is one threat; multi-factor authentication and recovery contacts reduce the risk.", "Pick one account you own. Write one asset, one possible threat, one impact, and one practical safeguard.", "What is the difference between a threat and a risk?", "A threat is a possible cause of harm; risk combines its likelihood and impact in a specific context."),
                lesson("Confidentiality, integrity, availability", "Beginner", "Confidentiality means information is seen only by authorized people. Integrity means it stays accurate and changes are traceable. Availability means people can access it when needed.|Controls can support more than one goal. Backups support availability and integrity; access rules support confidentiality and can also protect integrity. A design often balances these goals rather than maximizing one at any cost.", "A clinic protects patient records from casual viewing, records who updates them, and keeps a tested backup for emergencies.", "For a photo-sharing account, name one risk to each of the three goals.", "Which goal is most directly supported by verifying a backup can be restored?", "Availability, with integrity also supported when the restored data is checked for correctness."),
                lesson("Identity and access basics", "Beginner", "Authentication checks who is signing in; authorization decides what that identity may do. Multi-factor authentication combines different evidence, such as a password and a hardware key or authenticator approval.|Unique passwords prevent one breached service from unlocking unrelated accounts. A password manager helps create and store them. Recovery methods and session controls matter too: secure the email account used to reset other accounts.", "A staff member who reads reports may not need permission to export or delete them.", "Review one account's active sessions and recovery methods using its official settings page. Do not share a one-time code with anyone.", "Does a successful login automatically mean the user may access every record?", "No. Authorization should check the user's role and the specific action or record."),
                lesson("Updates and backups", "Beginner", "Updates close known weaknesses and improve reliability. Backups let you recover from deletion, device loss, or ransomware, but only if they are separate enough from the problem and can actually be restored.|A backup plan should name what is copied, how often, who can access it, how long it is kept, and how recovery is tested. Encrypt backups that contain private information and protect the recovery keys.", "A laptop backup that remains permanently connected and writable may be damaged along with the laptop. A protected offline or versioned copy reduces that risk.", "List your important files and how you would restore them to a replacement device. Test one harmless file restore.", "Why is a restore test part of a backup plan?", "It proves the data and recovery steps work before an emergency."),
                lesson("Everyday risk awareness", "Beginner", "Unexpected urgency, requests to bypass policy, and unusual payment or data requests deserve a pause. Verify using a contact channel you already trust, not the number or link inside the suspicious message.|People are part of the defense system. Make reporting easy and respectful so someone can ask for help early. Avoid blaming people who report a mistake; quick reporting often limits impact.", "If a message claims your manager needs gift cards immediately, call the manager using a saved work number before acting.", "Practice a neutral verification sentence: 'I will confirm this request using our usual channel before I act.'", "Why should you avoid using the contact details in a suspicious message?", "The message may control those details and route you back to the person pretending to be trusted."),
                lesson("Security recap", "Intermediate", "A strong foundation combines identity protection, updates, backups, access limits, and a plan for unusual events. Controls should be reviewed and adapted as the person or organization changes.|Do not buy tools before defining the problem. A clear policy, staff training, and a tested recovery process can matter more than a dashboard full of alerts that nobody owns.", "For a family photo archive, unique accounts, automatic updates, and a tested backup work together as a simple defense plan.", "Choose one small safeguard to improve this week. Record what success looks like and when you will check it.", "Why should a security plan name an owner for each action?", "Without an owner, an important task can remain undone because everyone assumes someone else will do it."),
                lesson("Threat modeling and risk ranking", "Advanced", "Threat modeling is a structured way to ask what a system protects, where data moves, who can interact with it, and how it could fail. A simple model can use assets, trust boundaries, threats, controls, and assumptions.|Rank risks by impact and likelihood, then include uncertainty. The purpose is not to predict every attack; it is to make design choices visible and prioritize improvements that reduce meaningful harm.", "For an online class portal, draw the learner, browser, application, database, and administrator. Mark where authentication and authorization are checked.", "Create a one-page threat model for a fictional class portal. Include one privacy risk, one availability risk, a mitigation, and an assumption.", "What is a useful output of a threat model?", "A prioritized set of risks, assumptions, and design actions that the team can review."),
                lesson("Cryptography: hash, encrypt, sign", "Advanced", "Encryption protects confidentiality by making data unreadable without a key. A cryptographic hash creates a fixed-size digest used to check integrity; secure password storage uses a slow, salted password-hashing scheme rather than a plain fast hash.|A digital signature helps verify that data was signed by a holder of a private key and was not altered. These tools solve different problems. Use maintained libraries and protocols; designing your own cryptography is unsafe.", "A download page can publish a trusted checksum so a user can check whether a file changed. A checksum alone does not prove who created it unless the source is authenticated.", "Sort three goals: hide a message, detect a file change, and verify a publisher. Match each to encryption, hashing, or a signature.", "Should passwords be stored with reversible encryption?", "Usually no. Store them using a dedicated, salted password-hashing function so the original password cannot simply be recovered."),
                lesson("Govern, identify, protect, detect, respond, recover", "Advanced", "NIST's Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 organizes outcomes into six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover. Govern sets direction and accountability; Identify understands assets and risk.|Protect reduces exposure; Detect finds possible events; Respond coordinates action; Recover restores services and learns from the event. These functions work together continuously. They are outcomes to organize work, not a checklist that guarantees safety.", "A small library can govern who approves access, identify critical records, protect them with MFA, detect unusual sign-ins, respond using a contact plan, and recover from a tested backup.", "Map one safeguard you already use to a function, then write one missing action for a different function.", "Why does the framework include Govern?", "Security needs risk ownership and decision-making, not only technical controls.")
            ]
        },
        {
            id: "web-safety",
            title: "Web Safety Basics",
            category: "SECURITY",
            description: "Understand the web request lifecycle, account sessions, safe application design, and today's common web risks.",
            diagram: { kind: "flow", accent: "#57dfd2", caption: "Encryption protects the connection; authorization protects each action.", nodes: ["BROWSER", "HTTPS / TLS", "WEB APP", "DATA STORE"] },
            references: [{ label: "OWASP Top 10:2025", url: "https://top10.owasp.org/2025/" }],
            lessons: [
                lesson("How a browser reaches a site", "Beginner", "A domain name is easier to remember than an address. DNS helps find the server, and the browser then requests the page and its resources. The address bar shows the site name that receives your information.|A page may load code, images, fonts, and APIs from more than one origin. Reviewing the domain before signing in is an important habit, especially when you reached the page through a message.", "Typing a bank's saved bookmark is safer than following a surprise link that claims your account is locked.", "Open a familiar public site and identify the exact domain before interacting. Do not enter credentials during this exercise.", "What should you check before entering account information?", "The exact domain, the reason for the request, and whether you reached it through a trusted path."),
                lesson("HTTPS and secure connections", "Beginner", "HTTPS uses TLS to protect data in transit and help your browser verify the server endpoint. The certificate is checked against the domain and trusted certificate authorities.|A secure connection does not prove the organization is legitimate or that the page is free of unsafe content. Attackers can also register domains and obtain valid certificates. Treat the address and context as separate checks.", "A convincing fake shop can use HTTPS. The connection may be encrypted while the business is still fraudulent.", "Compare the domain name in a bookmark with the one in an example message. Look for subtle extra words or misspellings.", "Does the padlock mean a site is trustworthy?", "No. It indicates a protected connection to a domain, not that the domain owner is honest."),
                lesson("Safer account habits", "Beginner", "Use a unique password for each important account, store it in a reputable password manager, and enable a strong second factor where available. Prefer phishing-resistant hardware security keys or passkeys when supported.|Review active sessions, recovery email, and phone settings periodically. A password reset email is powerful, so protect the inbox that receives it with its own strong sign-in controls.", "If a shopping site is breached, a unique password prevents that exposed password from opening your email account.", "Choose one account and check its recovery options through the official app or typed-in site address.", "Why use different passwords across services?", "A breach on one service then does not automatically give access to your other accounts."),
                lesson("Safer browsing", "Beginner", "Install apps and extensions only from sources you trust. Extensions may read or change page data depending on the permissions they receive, so remove ones you no longer use.|Keep the browser and operating system updated. Be cautious with downloads that demand urgent action, disable protections, or ask you to paste commands. When in doubt, close the page and verify through an official support channel.", "A simple PDF viewer extension may ask for access to every site even though it only needs to open local files. That permission deserves scrutiny.", "Review installed browser extensions and remove only ones you recognize as unnecessary. Check each extension's publisher and requested access.", "Why should an extension's permissions match its purpose?", "Excess access increases the possible impact if the extension is compromised or behaves unexpectedly."),
                lesson("Web safety recap", "Intermediate", "A safe browsing decision checks the domain, connection, request, permissions, and expected action. If any element is surprising, pause and navigate to the service independently.|HTTPS, a familiar logo, or a message from a known contact is not enough on its own. Attackers can imitate branding or compromise accounts. Verify important requests out of band.", "A password reset request you did not initiate is a reason to open the service using a saved bookmark and review the account.", "Make a five-point personal checklist for links, logins, downloads, and extensions.", "What is an out-of-band check?", "Confirming a request through a separate trusted channel, such as calling a saved number."),
                lesson("Cookies, sessions, and sign-in", "Intermediate", "After you sign in, a site often uses a session token to remember that your browser is authenticated. Cookies can carry this token; because possession may grant access, session tokens must be protected like credentials.|Secure web apps limit cookie access, use HTTPS, rotate or expire sessions appropriately, and offer a way to sign out or revoke sessions. Clearing cookies can sign you out, but does not fix a compromised account by itself.", "A shared computer should not retain an account session after the learner finishes. Signing out and closing the browser reduce accidental access.", "On your own account, review active sessions and sign out of a device you no longer use.", "Why should a session token be treated like a password?", "Someone who obtains it may be able to act as the signed-in user until it expires or is revoked."),
                lesson("Access control and authorization", "Advanced", "Authentication answers 'who are you?' Authorization answers 'may you perform this action on this resource?' Every server-side action should check identity, role, and ownership; hiding a button in the browser is not enough.|Use deny-by-default rules and grant the smallest set of permissions needed. Test both allowed and denied cases using accounts and records you control. Access-control mistakes are a major category in the OWASP Top 10.", "A learner should be able to read their own progress but not another learner's progress by changing a page address.", "Design a permission table for a fictional course app: learner, instructor, administrator. List allowed actions for each role.", "Where must an authorization check occur?", "On the trusted server or backend before data is returned or changed, not only in the user interface."),
                lesson("OWASP Top 10:2025 overview", "Advanced", "OWASP's 2025 awareness list highlights broad application risk areas including broken access control, security misconfiguration, software supply-chain failures, cryptographic failures, injection, insecure design, authentication failures, integrity failures, logging gaps, and mishandled errors.|The list is a learning map, not a complete security standard or a ranking for every application. Use it to ask design questions, then adapt controls to the system, data, and threat model. Fix root causes rather than hiding symptoms.", "A team that updates a vulnerable dependency should also check how it was introduced, tested, and deployed, not only patch one server.", "Choose one OWASP category and write one safe prevention question a developer could ask during a design review.", "Why should teams not treat the OWASP Top 10 as a complete checklist?", "It is an awareness starting point; each application's risks and controls depend on its context."),
                lesson("Secure design and dependencies", "Advanced", "Secure design starts before code is written: define trust boundaries, minimize collected data, validate decisions on the server, and plan safe failure behavior. Dependencies should come from known sources and be updated through a reviewable process.|Pin and inventory important packages, review advisories, protect build credentials, and separate development from production access. A supply-chain issue can enter through code, packages, build tools, or deployment steps.", "A class project can list its third-party libraries and update them after reading the package's official release notes.", "Create a simple inventory of dependencies for a project you own. Remove only packages the project owner approves.", "Why keep a dependency inventory?", "It helps identify what may be affected when a vulnerability or end-of-support notice appears.")
            ]
        },
        {
            id: "threat-awareness",
            title: "Threat Awareness",
            category: "PRACTICE",
            description: "Spot manipulation, protect accounts, and respond calmly to suspicious messages or security events.",
            diagram: { kind: "steps", accent: "#ff89a7", caption: "Slow the moment down; verify through a channel you trust.", nodes: ["PAUSE", "INSPECT", "VERIFY", "REPORT"] },
            references: [{ label: "NIST Incident Response guidance", url: "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final" }],
            lessons: [
                lesson("Spotting suspicious messages", "Beginner", "Unexpected urgency, secrecy, unusual payment requests, or a demand to bypass normal approval can signal manipulation. One clue alone does not prove a message is malicious; context matters.|Check the actual sender address and whether the request matches the person's normal process. Do not let a polished logo or familiar name replace verification. Reporting a suspicious message helps protect others.", "A message that says 'I am in a meeting; send the code now' should be checked through a known phone number or in-person conversation.", "Practice naming two reasons a fictional message feels unusual and one trusted way to verify it.", "What is safer than replying to a suspicious message?", "Contact the person or organization through a channel you already trust."),
                lesson("Links and attachments", "Beginner", "A link's displayed text can differ from its destination. Shortened links hide details, while attachments may exploit outdated software or trick you into enabling active content.|For important services, use a bookmark or type the known address yourself. Do not open unexpected files just to inspect them. If a work file is suspicious, follow the organization's reporting process rather than forwarding it to others.", "A delivery text can imitate a real courier while using a domain with extra words or a different ending.", "Use a fictional example to identify the displayed domain and explain why a saved bookmark is a safer route.", "What should you do with an unexpected attachment?", "Verify through a trusted channel and use the official reporting process; do not open it out of curiosity."),
                lesson("Social engineering signals", "Intermediate", "Social engineering targets human trust and time pressure. A caller may impersonate support, a bank, a colleague, or an authority figure and ask for codes, remote access, or exceptions.|Legitimate support should not ask for your one-time sign-in code or pressure you to install remote-control software from a message. End the interaction and call an independently verified number if the request is unusual.", "A fake support caller asks you to read the code that just arrived on your phone. That code is intended for you; do not share it.", "Practice a polite stop phrase: 'I will call back using the official number and verify this request.'", "Why are one-time codes sensitive?", "They can complete a sign-in or approval step, so sharing one may hand control of an account to someone else."),
                lesson("What to do when something feels wrong", "Intermediate", "Stop interacting and avoid sending more information. If you clicked or entered a password, visit the official service from a clean route, change the affected password, revoke unknown sessions, and contact the provider or IT support.|Report promptly with the time, account or device affected, what happened, and any actions already taken. Preserve the original message when policy permits, but do not forward dangerous files broadly or delete evidence needed by responders.", "If you entered a work password into a look-alike site, tell IT immediately and change it through the official sign-in page.", "Write the first three people or official channels you would contact for a personal account incident.", "Why should you report even if you are unsure?", "Early reporting gives support teams a chance to contain impact before more accounts or devices are affected."),
                lesson("QR codes, look-alike domains, and AI impersonation", "Advanced", "QR codes hide a link until scanned, and convincing text or voice can now be generated quickly. Treat the destination and request as more important than how familiar or polished the message looks.|Look for domain changes, unexpected login prompts, and pressure to keep the request secret. Verify payments, access changes, and urgent instructions with a separate trusted channel, especially when the voice sounds familiar but the request is unusual.", "A voice note that sounds like a relative but urgently requests a transfer should be confirmed by calling the relative at a saved number.", "Create a verification checklist for a QR-code payment: expected merchant, exact domain, amount, and independent confirmation.", "Can a realistic voice prove who sent a request?", "No. Voice and video can be imitated; verify high-impact requests independently."),
                lesson("Account takeover: contain and recover", "Advanced", "If an account may be taken over, use a trusted device and official site. Change the password, revoke unknown sessions and recovery methods, enable a strong second factor, and check forwarding rules or connected apps.|If the account controls other services, secure the email and identity provider first. Contact the organization through official support. Save relevant alerts and timestamps; do not share recovery codes with helpers or people who contact you unexpectedly.", "After a suspicious email sign-in, secure the email account first because it may be able to reset other passwords.", "Prepare a recovery checklist for a fictional learning account, including who to contact and how to revoke sessions.", "Why secure the email account early?", "It often receives password resets and can be used to regain control of other accounts."),
                lesson("Ransomware resilience", "Advanced", "Ransomware can make files unavailable and may also involve data theft. Prevention includes timely updates, least privilege, protected backups, and a clear reporting route; no single control eliminates risk.|If a device is affected, follow the organization's incident plan. Disconnecting a device may be appropriate under local instructions, but do not improvise changes that could destroy evidence. Use tested backups only after responders confirm the environment is safe to restore.", "A versioned backup that cannot be changed by an everyday account can help recover after malicious encryption.", "Draw a recovery plan for a small club's shared files: owner, backup copy, restore test, and contact person.", "Why should recovery wait until the environment is understood?", "Restoring too early can reintroduce the problem or overwrite useful evidence."),
                lesson("Incident response: prepare to recover", "Advanced", "Incident response is an organizational capability, not just a technical action. Preparation defines roles and contacts; detection and analysis establish what happened; response limits impact; recovery restores services and captures lessons.|NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3 connects incident response to broader cybersecurity risk management and the CSF 2.0. Good plans include decision authority, communications, evidence handling, and exercises. Use your organization's current playbook during a real event.", "A school can rehearse who contacts the provider, who informs families, and who approves restoring a service after an account compromise.", "Create a tabletop scenario for a fictional club and list one decision for preparation, response, and recovery. Do not use a real account or system.", "What should an incident playbook clarify before a crisis?", "Roles, escalation contacts, decision authority, communications, evidence handling, and recovery steps.")
            ]
        },
        {
            id: "ethical-practice",
            title: "Ethical Security Practice",
            category: "PRACTICE",
            description: "Build practical security judgment through explicit permission, isolated labs, careful notes, and responsible reporting.",
            diagram: { kind: "scope", accent: "#ffb15c", caption: "Every exercise stays inside a written, authorized scope.", nodes: ["PERMISSION", "SAFE LAB", "MINIMAL PROOF", "REPORT"] },
            references: [{ label: "CISA vulnerability disclosure guidance", url: "https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/vulnerability-disclosure-policy-vdp-platform" }],
            lessons: [
                lesson("Permission and scope", "Beginner", "Only assess systems you own or have explicit written permission to test. Permission should name the systems, allowed activities, time window, data rules, and a contact for concerns.|A public address or an unprotected page is not permission. If the scope is unclear, stop and ask the owner. Scope is a boundary that protects people, systems, and your learning.", "A class assignment may authorize testing a local virtual machine but exclude the school's Wi-Fi, shared accounts, and public cloud services.", "Write a one-sentence scope for a toy local lab. Include one thing that is explicitly out of scope.", "Does being able to reach a system mean you may test it?", "No. Reachability is not authorization. Get explicit permission for the exact activity."),
                lesson("Safe practice environments", "Beginner", "Use training labs that clearly authorize the activity, or a virtual machine on a network you control. Keep practice data fictional and take a snapshot before experiments so you can restore the lab.|Never connect an intentionally vulnerable training system to a public network. Separate it from personal files and accounts. Follow the lab provider's rules, and stop if an exercise asks you to target an unrelated service.", "A host-only virtual network can let two local training machines communicate without exposing them to other devices.", "Before a lab, record the allowed targets, network mode, reset steps, and stop condition.", "Why isolate an intentionally vulnerable lab?", "Isolation prevents the exercise from affecting real devices, accounts, and networks."),
                lesson("Responsible reporting", "Intermediate", "If you discover a possible weakness, stop at the minimum evidence needed to describe it. Do not access other people's data, change records, or continue testing beyond the permission you have.|Use the owner's published reporting route. Include the affected component, safe reproduction context, impact, timestamps, and remediation ideas. Redact secrets and personal data; coordinate disclosure with the owner.", "A report can explain that a user-owned test record was visible to another test account without collecting anyone else's records.", "Draft a report using a fictional issue and placeholder data. Include what you did not access or change.", "Why should you minimize evidence collection?", "It reduces harm and privacy exposure while still giving the owner enough information to verify the issue."),
                lesson("Keep learning ethically", "Intermediate", "Ethics is a practical skill. Before an exercise, confirm ownership or written permission, read the rules, identify data boundaries, and know how to contact the lab owner.|A technique learned in a course should not be applied to a real system just because it is technically possible. Professional judgment includes refusing an unsafe request and proposing an authorized alternative.", "If a practice page links to an unrelated public server, stop and ask the course administrator rather than following it.", "Make a short personal checklist for permission, scope, data safety, and stop conditions.", "What should you do when an exercise falls outside its stated scope?", "Stop, avoid further access, and ask the authorized owner or instructor for clarification."),
                lesson("Ethics recap", "Intermediate", "A responsible learner protects people while learning. Written permission, least-impact methods, careful notes, and honest reporting are part of the technical work.|If you accidentally cross a boundary, stop immediately and report what happened accurately. Do not hide activity, delete logs, or continue to 'prove' the issue.", "A good lab note says what was authorized, what was observed, and when the exercise ended.", "Write a short lab note for a fictional exercise that records scope and a clear stop time.", "What is the right response after an accidental out-of-scope action?", "Stop, preserve the facts, notify the owner or instructor promptly, and follow their guidance."),
                lesson("Write rules of engagement", "Advanced", "Rules of engagement turn permission into an operational plan. They define scope, dates, allowed methods, rate limits, accounts, data handling, emergency contacts, and conditions that require work to stop.|Both tester and owner should understand the plan before work begins. A change in ownership, system, or purpose requires renewed approval. Keep the signed authorization with the exercise notes.", "A lab rule may permit reviewing a local sample app but prohibit sending messages, accessing real customer data, or testing the provider's infrastructure.", "Draft rules for a fictional training app. Add at least two explicit exclusions and a contact for unexpected behavior.", "Why put exclusions in writing?", "They prevent assumptions about which systems or actions are allowed."),
                lesson("Evidence handling and lab notes", "Advanced", "A useful note records date and time, environment, scope version, exact observation, and the next safe step. Avoid collecting extra data. Store notes where only authorized people can read them and delete temporary material according to the rules.|When evidence matters, preserve its original state and document any copy or transformation. Never alter system logs to hide a test. For a learning lab, use synthetic data and keep screenshots free of tokens or personal information.", "A screenshot of a toy app can show a redacted sample record and the browser URL without exposing an actual person's information.", "Create a sample evidence log with fictional values and a retention date.", "What makes a lab note reproducible without being excessive?", "Clear environment and timing, the minimal observation, and the exact authorized scope."),
                lesson("Write a useful vulnerability report", "Advanced", "A clear report includes a short title, affected component, scope, impact, minimal reproduction steps, evidence with sensitive values removed, and a suggested fix. Separate what you observed from what you infer.|Use calm, factual language and provide the owner time to investigate. Do not publish details or threaten disclosure to force a response. Follow the organization's disclosure policy and coordinate any public write-up.", "A report might state that a fictional test role can view a record outside its assigned class, then recommend a server-side ownership check.", "Write a one-page report about a fictional access-control problem using only synthetic accounts and data.", "Why separate observed facts from impact assumptions?", "It helps the owner verify the issue and decide severity without treating speculation as proof."),
                lesson("Capstone: defend a small learning site", "Advanced", "Combine the paths in a fictional academy: identify learner accounts and progress records, draw the browser-to-server data flow, define roles, and list likely failure modes.|Prioritize practical controls: unique sign-in, server-side authorization, protected backups, dependency review, alert ownership, and a recovery contact. State assumptions and limits. A useful capstone is a defensible plan, not an unauthorized attack.", "A learner may read their own progress; an instructor may manage assigned course content; an administrator can manage the platform with audited access.", "Create a one-page diagram and risk table for the fictional academy. Include one test that uses only synthetic users in a local lab.", "What makes the capstone ethical as well as useful?", "It uses a fictional system and synthetic data, states scope, and focuses on defensive improvements.")
            ]
        },
        {
            id: "algorithms",
            title: "Algorithms & Data Structures",
            category: "FOUNDATION",
            description: "Build problem-solving foundations with complexity, arrays, hashing, search, sorting, trees, graphs, and dynamic programming.",
            diagram: { kind: "flow", accent: "#66d6ff", caption: "Turn a clear problem statement into a tested, efficient solution.", nodes: ["UNDERSTAND", "CHOOSE DATA", "DESIGN", "MEASURE"] },
            references: [],
            lessons: [
                lesson("Think in steps: algorithms and data", "Beginner", "An algorithm is a finite sequence of steps that turns an input into an output. A data structure is a way to arrange information so those steps can use it. Good solutions start by stating the input, the expected result, and any limits.|A clear plan is often easier to improve than code written from a guess. First solve a tiny example by hand, then describe the repeated steps in plain language, and only then choose a programming language.", "To find the largest value in a list, keep a current maximum, inspect each value once, and replace the maximum whenever a larger value appears.", "Write the steps for finding the smallest of five numbers you create yourself. Check the result by hand on a second example.", "What should you identify before choosing a data structure?", "The input, the required output, the operations you need, and the size limits."),
                lesson("Time complexity and Big O", "Beginner", "Time complexity describes how the amount of work grows as input size grows. Big O is a useful upper-growth description: O(1) stays roughly constant, O(n) grows with each item, and O(n squared) often comes from comparing every pair.|Big O ignores machine speed and small constants. It helps compare approaches for large inputs, but measurements on realistic data still matter. A nested loop is not automatically O(n squared); inspect how many times each loop actually runs.", "Scanning an array once is O(n). Comparing every pair of different positions is O(n squared), so the second approach can become slow much sooner.", "Count the number of comparisons in a loop over 8 items, then estimate the count for 16 items.", "If input size doubles, what usually happens to the work in an O(n) scan?", "It roughly doubles, assuming each item takes a constant amount of work."),
                lesson("Space complexity and trade-offs", "Beginner", "Space complexity describes how extra memory grows with input size. A solution can use constant extra space, O(1), or allocate a structure that grows with the input, O(n). The input itself is sometimes counted separately, so always state what your estimate includes.|Faster and smaller are not always the same goal. A lookup table may use extra memory to avoid repeatedly scanning a list. Choose a trade-off that fits the problem and its constraints.", "A frequency map for a list of distinct words can store one entry per word. It uses more memory than a scan, but repeated lookups can be much faster.", "For a task you invent, write one option that uses extra storage and one that recomputes values. Compare their time and memory needs.", "Does O(1) extra space mean the program uses no memory at all?", "No. It means the extra memory stays bounded as input size grows; the program and input still occupy memory."),
                lesson("Arrays and indexed access", "Beginner", "An array stores items in order and lets you access a position by index. Indexed reads are typically O(1), while searching for a value is O(n). Inserting near the beginning may require shifting many later items.|Arrays are useful when order matters and you often read or update known positions. Before using an index, check that it lies within the valid range. Different languages may use zero-based or one-based indexing.", "To compute a running total, start with zero and add each array value once. This takes O(n) time and O(1) extra space.", "Create a short list of numbers. Find its length, first item, last item, and total; test what happens for an empty list.", "Why is finding an item by its index usually faster than searching by its value?", "The index points directly to a position, while a value search may need to inspect items one by one."),
                lesson("Strings and character counts", "Beginner", "A string is an ordered sequence of characters. Some languages treat strings as immutable, so changing one character may create a new string. Character encoding also matters: a visible symbol can contain more than one code unit.|For tasks such as checking anagrams, counting characters is often more useful than sorting the whole string. Normalize case or spacing only when the problem says those differences should be ignored.", "For the strings “listen” and “silent”, a frequency table can show that each letter appears the same number of times.", "Write a small function that counts letters in a sentence you create. Decide explicitly whether uppercase and lowercase count as the same.", "What must an anagram check compare?", "The same character counts, after applying the exact normalization rules in the problem."),
                lesson("Hash maps and fast lookup", "Beginner", "A hash map stores key-value pairs and usually provides fast average lookup, insertion, and update. It is a natural choice for counting, remembering earlier values, or mapping an identifier to details.|Hash maps use extra memory, and their average speed is not a guarantee for every possible implementation or input. Keys should have stable equality and hashing behavior. When order matters, choose a map that preserves or sorts keys as required.", "In a two-sum task, store numbers already seen. For each new value, check whether the target minus that value is present.", "Count the frequency of a few words using a map, then explain what each key and value represents.", "What is one common time-memory trade-off when using a hash map?", "It uses extra memory to avoid repeating a slower search through prior items."),
                lesson("Linear and binary search", "Beginner", "Linear search checks items one at a time and works even when the data is unsorted. Binary search repeatedly halves the remaining range, but it requires sorted data and careful boundary rules.|When implementing binary search, decide whether the interval is closed or half-open and keep that rule consistent. Update the bounds so the search range gets strictly smaller on each step.", "Searching 1,000,000 sorted values with binary search takes about 20 comparisons because each step halves the range; linear search may inspect every value.", "Trace binary search on a sorted list you create. Record low, middle, and high after each comparison.", "What condition must usually be true before using binary search on an array?", "The array must be sorted according to the same ordering used by the search."),
                lesson("Sorting and choosing an approach", "Beginner", "Sorting rearranges data into a chosen order. Simple methods such as insertion sort are useful for learning and small nearly-sorted inputs; efficient comparison sorts commonly take O(n log n) time on large inputs.|A sort may be stable, preserving the relative order of equal keys, and may be in-place or allocate extra memory. In real programs, prefer the language's well-tested sort unless you are learning an algorithm or have a specific requirement.", "To rank learners by score and then by name, define a comparator with both keys. A stable sort can preserve an earlier order among exact ties.", "Sort five values manually, then write down which comparisons and swaps you used.", "Name one property besides runtime that can matter when selecting a sorting method.", "Stability, extra memory use, input ordering, or whether the method fits the constraints."),
                lesson("Two pointers and sliding windows", "Intermediate", "Two-pointer methods keep two indices and move them according to a rule. They can solve sorted pair-sum or partition tasks in linear time. Sliding windows use a moving range to summarize contiguous data.|These patterns are efficient when the condition changes predictably as a pointer moves. For a variable-size window, expand to include new data and shrink while the constraint is broken. Always verify what the window includes at each step.", "For a sorted array, compare the values at the left and right ends to find a target sum. For a longest substring with a limit, grow a window and remove characters from the left when needed.", "Draw the pointer positions for an example array and annotate each move with the rule that allowed it.", "Why can two pointers be faster than checking every pair?", "Each pointer moves forward only a limited number of times, so the overall scan can be O(n) instead of O(n squared)."),
                lesson("Stacks and queues", "Beginner", "A stack uses last-in, first-out order; a queue uses first-in, first-out order. Stacks help with nested structures and undo history, while queues help process tasks in arrival order.|Both structures restrict which end you access. That simple rule makes their behavior predictable. A deque supports adding or removing at either end when a problem needs both.", "A stack can check balanced brackets: push each opening bracket, then match and remove it when a closing bracket appears.", "Trace a queue of three made-up support tickets. Show the order they leave when handled first-in, first-out.", "Which structure naturally models a browser back button?", "A stack, because the most recently visited page is usually the first one returned to."),
                lesson("Linked lists and pointer updates", "Intermediate", "A linked list stores nodes that point to other nodes. Inserting after a known node can be O(1), but finding the position usually takes O(n). Nodes are not necessarily next to one another in memory.|Updating links safely requires drawing the old and new connections. Keep a reference to the next node before changing a link, or part of the list may become unreachable. Arrays are often simpler when frequent indexed reads are needed.", "To reverse a list, walk through nodes while redirecting each next pointer to the previous node and keeping track of the remaining list.", "Draw four nodes and simulate reversing their links without running code.", "Why can inserting into the middle of a linked list still take O(n)?", "The program may need to traverse the list first to reach the insertion position."),
                lesson("Recursion and backtracking", "Intermediate", "A recursive function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version. It needs a base case that stops recursion and a progress rule that moves toward that case. Each call uses stack space.|Backtracking explores choices, then undoes a choice when that path cannot produce a valid solution. It can be exponential, so use it when the search space is manageable or add pruning and constraints.", "To list arrangements of a few distinct letters, choose one letter, recursively arrange the remainder, then restore the choice before trying another.", "Write the base case and the shrinking input for a recursive countdown. Trace calls for a small value.", "What can happen if a recursive call never approaches a base case?", "The calls can continue until the program runs out of stack space or another resource."),
                lesson("Trees and graph traversal", "Intermediate", "A tree organizes nodes in parent-child relationships and has no cycles. A graph is more general: nodes may connect in many patterns, including cycles. Breadth-first search explores by layers; depth-first search follows a branch before returning.|Breadth-first search with a queue finds a shortest path in an unweighted graph. Depth-first search with a stack or recursion is useful for reachability and structure checks. Track visited nodes to avoid repeating work in cyclic graphs.", "To explore rooms connected by hallways, model rooms as nodes and hallways as edges. A visited set stops the search from circling forever.", "Draw six nodes with a cycle. Write the visit order for BFS from one node and mark each node when it enters the queue.", "What data structure does BFS commonly use?", "A queue, so nodes are processed in the order they are discovered."),
                lesson("Greedy choices and proof", "Advanced", "A greedy algorithm makes the best-looking local choice at each step. It is efficient when that local choice can be shown to lead to a globally optimal result.|A plausible strategy is not a proof. Look for an exchange argument, an invariant, or a known theorem. If a small counterexample defeats the greedy choice, a different method such as dynamic programming may be needed.", "For selecting the maximum number of non-overlapping meetings, sorting by finish time and choosing the earliest finish has a proof based on exchanging the first selected meeting.", "Invent a small scheduling example and compare the earliest-finish rule with choosing the shortest meeting. Try to find a counterexample to the wrong rule.", "Why should you test a greedy rule on small counterexamples?", "A quick counterexample can expose a local choice that does not guarantee the best overall solution."),
                lesson("Dynamic programming and repeated work", "Advanced", "Dynamic programming organizes problems with overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure. Memoization stores results from recursive calls; tabulation fills a table from smaller states upward.|The hardest design step is defining a state that contains exactly the information needed for the remaining problem. Then write the transition, base cases, and answer location. Check that the table order computes dependencies before they are used.", "For a staircase with one- or two-step moves, the number of ways to reach step n depends on the counts for n minus one and n minus two.", "Write a recurrence and a small table for the staircase problem for n from zero to six. Explain each cell in a sentence.", "What is a dynamic programming state?", "A compact description of a subproblem whose stored answer can be reused by larger subproblems.")
            ]
        }
    ];
})();

